WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
This is The Spread, your energy
markets briefing for Wednesday,

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:05.360
the eleventh of November.

3
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.320
This episode is written and read with AI.

4
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.680
Ukrainian drone strikes have knocked
out roughly a third of Russia's

5
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:16.200
refining capacity, just as Moscow is
trying to send more diesel abroad.

6
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.560
Meanwhile Brent briefly slipped below
ninety dollars on signs of de-escalation

7
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.240
with Iran, though freight costs and
fresh attacks on Gulf shipping are

8
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.520
keeping delivered crude expensive.

9
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.960
On the markets. At the Asian open,
Brent was at ninety-one dollars sixty

10
00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.427
and West Texas Intermediate
at eighty-five dollars eighty.

11
00:00:34.453 --> 00:00:38.160
The American Petroleum Institute
estimates a nine million barrel crude

12
00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.360
build in the week to
the sixth of November,

13
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.773
reversing a draw the week before.

14
00:00:42.907 --> 00:00:45.920
Another two point seven million
barrels left the Strategic Petroleum

15
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.240
Reserve, and the Institute also
reports a distillate build,

16
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.520
without giving a figure. European gas
storage stood at seventy-two percent

17
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.520
full on the ninth of November,
according to Gas Infrastructure Europe.

18
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.600
Starting in Russia. Two industry
sources told Reuters that Ukrainian

19
00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:05.360
drone attacks since late October have
cut the country's refining capacity

20
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.760
by about thirty percent. Nine of
Russia's thirty-five major refineries

21
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.880
were disrupted over the past week.

22
00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.520
Together they account for about two
point two million barrels a day of

23
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.173
nameplate capacity.

24
00:01:17.227 --> 00:01:19.387
The timing is awkward for Moscow.

25
00:01:19.413 --> 00:01:23.120
It is trying to raise diesel exports
under an agreement with Washington,

26
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.240
so fewer barrels are being made just
as more are being promised abroad.

27
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.493
That matters for anyone
buying distillates,

28
00:01:29.547 --> 00:01:32.040
which remain the main
prop for refining margins,

29
00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.000
according to a note from
the analytics firm Kpler.

30
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.693
Crude that can't be refined at
home may end up exported instead,

31
00:01:38.827 --> 00:01:40.640
provided the ports are intact.

32
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.480
The reports give no timelines
for damage repair or restarts.

33
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.320
Meanwhile, the first Russian diesel
cargo bound for the US has stopped

34
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.320
off the American east
coast, Reuters reported.

35
00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.520
Its operator says no
destination has been decided.

36
00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.760
The US energy secretary says he's
confident it will eventually be delivered.

37
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.560
Turning to crude prices. Front-month
Brent touched eighty-nine dollars

38
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.600
seventy early Tuesday in Europe,
according to OilPrice dot com.

39
00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.800
That was its first move
below ninety since August.

40
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.653
Crude has now fallen almost thirty
dollars a barrel since the midterms.

41
00:02:13.707 --> 00:02:15.440
The trigger was President Trump.

42
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.760
After meeting Qatar's prime minister,
he said he's keen to pursue diplomacy

43
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.920
and wind down the war, and signalled
no strikes on Iran for now,

44
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:28.000
Rigzone reported. There were also
reports that Washington would let Iran

45
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.960
enrich uranium to almost four percent.

46
00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:33.760
Official sources have not confirmed that.

47
00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.680
The catch for refiners is that physical
flows are recovering faster than

48
00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.320
costs are falling. Vessel tracking has
shown Gulf exports close to pre-war

49
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.480
levels for over a month, and Middle
East benchmarks are weakening.

50
00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.480
But war cover, insurance and record
tanker rates still keep delivered

51
00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.400
costs high, and rates for very large
crude carriers from the Gulf to

52
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.720
China have dipped only marginally.

53
00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.760
China is buying again. Its October
crude imports came to about nine point

54
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.560
nine million barrels a day,
according to Chinese customs data.

55
00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.840
That's the highest since March and up
nine point four percent on September.

56
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.760
It's still thirteen point three
percent below October last year.

57
00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.480
For American drivers, there's some relief.

58
00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:20.800
Regular gasoline averaged just under
four dollars a gallon on Tuesday,

59
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.280
according to the American
Automobile Association.

60
00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.920
That's the first reading
below four dollars since July.

61
00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.960
Over in the Gulf, the
attacks haven't stopped.

62
00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.000
A vessel belonging to ADNOC Logistics
and Services was damaged in an

63
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.280
attack in UAE territorial
waters on Tuesday,

64
00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.920
according to gCaptain and World Oil.

65
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Separately, the United Kingdom Maritime
Trade Operations centre reported

66
00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:48.240
an apparent projectile strike on a
tanker near the Strait of Hormuz.

67
00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.240
In Iraqi Kurdistan, production at
the Khor Mor gas facility is still

68
00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.960
suspended after a suspected drone attack
and fire on the eighth of November.

69
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.000
The operators, Dana Gas
and Crescent Petroleum,

70
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.480
say damage is still being assessed.

71
00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:06.160
The Houthis say they hit pumping
stations on Saudi Arabia's East-West

72
00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.000
pipeline, along with Abqaiq and
Riyadh's King Khalid airport.

73
00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.600
That is a claim only, and Riyadh
has not acknowledged any attacks.

74
00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.360
The pipeline feeds Yanbu, the Saudi
export outlet that bypasses Hormuz.

75
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.680
Shipowners and insurers are looking
for ways to keep tankers moving.

76
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.000
The Saudi shipping company Bahri says
it is sending tankers through Hormuz

77
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.480
in coordination with the US military.

78
00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.320
And President Macron is discussing
a publicly backed vehicle with Saudi

79
00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.400
Arabia and other Gulf states to take
on part of the war-risk insurance

80
00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.200
for Hormuz transits, Rigzone reported.

81
00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.440
The Gulf states have not commented.

82
00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:48.160
Now to gas. Qatar's LNG loadings
have picked up over recent weeks to

83
00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.440
around half of pre-war
levels, according to Reuters.

84
00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:55.600
Ras Laffan remains under force
majeure, which leaves buyers in Europe

85
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:59.040
and Asia still short of what
they had before the war.

86
00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.480
Thirty-one LNG tankers
crossed Hormuz in October,

87
00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.760
up from twenty-seven in
September, according to Kpler.

88
00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.440
That's the most since February, and
most of those cargoes loaded at Ras

89
00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.920
Laffan. Two were moved onto other
tankers after clearing the strait.

90
00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:17.840
In the US, a warm start to
November is easing the picture.

91
00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.480
The EIA now expects almost seventy
billion cubic feet more gas in storage

92
00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:25.440
at the end of the month
than it forecast in October.

93
00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.080
What to watch next. The EIA's weekly
inventory report comes out today,

94
00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.240
covering the week to
the sixth of November,

95
00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:35.520
and will show whether the Institute's
crude and distillate builds hold

96
00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:39.520
up. Today is also the final day
of the talks between the US,

97
00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.840
Ukraine and Russia in Abu Dhabi.

98
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.280
And the Jones Act emergency
waiver expires on Sunday,

99
00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:46.480
the fifteenth.

100
00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.240
That's the brief. Sources for everything
you heard are in the show notes.
